# Shared semantic screen rendering

Both screen adapters use accepted publication contributions and the whole-block
syntax emitter. Only semantic reference ranges become links; HTML is never
rescanned for tags. This module has no filesystem or VS Code dependency.


The adapter chooses destinations without changing logical identity. Fixed-width
IDs are shared with LaTeX; URL components, labels and attributes are escaped
separately. Preview supplies a synchronous lookup of asynchronously resolved URLs.

```ts : <<screen renderer imports>>=
import MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
import Token = require('markdown-it/lib/token');
import { posix } from 'path';
import type { PublicationModel, PublicationContribution } from './engine';
import type { PublicationAnnotations, PublicationTerm, PublicationNote, PublicationTermUse } from './publication-annotations';
import { parseAnnotationBody, resolveTermHref } from './publication-annotations';
import { highlightContribution } from './publication-highlight';
import { explorationPayload, explorationBrowserScript } from './publication-exploration';
import { parsePublicationReference, publicationReferenceTarget } from './publication-references';
const escapeHtml = new MarkdownIt().utils.escapeHtml;
export type ScreenFormat = 'html' | 'preview';
export type PreviewDestination = (target: string) => string | undefined;
```

Relative links work from nested chapters and percent-encode filenames (including
spaces, percent signs, hashes and colons). A leading dot prevents a filename from
being interpreted as a URI scheme. Same-document destinations are native anchors.
Cross-document preview links use only the extension's capability adapter. Pending
or failed resolutions stay text: native line-link fallback can create divergent
fragment-qualified documents in the host. Logical identity remains in
`data-literate-target`, independent of the opaque session URL.

Screen destination adaptation is presented in [Publication on screen](publication_on_screen.html).

Syntax is intersected with semantic spans, never used to discover them. Hiding
comment-only syntax retains the existing screen policy, but references inside
comments remain navigable. Unknown languages stay plain; the publication adapter
checks the pinned emitter's shape and exact text reconstruction.

```ts : <<screen syntax rendering>>=
export function renderScreenCode(part: PublicationContribution, model: PublicationModel, format: ScreenFormat,
    destination?: PreviewDestination): string {
    const language = format === 'preview' ? previewLanguage(part.language) : part.language;
    const display = highlightContribution(language === part.language ? part : { ...part, language }, true);
    const syntax = screenSyntax(display);
    const index = screenIndex(model);
    let cursor = 0, output = '';
    <<screen overlay references>>
    return output + syntax(cursor, display.text.length);
}
```

A missing family never gets an invented destination. The tolerant screen mode
keeps incomplete editor text visible while the strict LaTeX path still diagnoses
unsupported references. References are rendered once even across syntax leaves.

```ts : <<screen overlay references>>=
for (const ref of display.references) {
    output += syntax(cursor, ref.start);
    const family = index.families.get(ref.target);
    const base = index.parts.get(family?.contributions[0] || '');
    const text = display.text.slice(ref.start, ref.end);
    const link = base ? screenLink(screenHref(part.definition.path, base.definition.path, ref.target, format, destination),
        `Definition of ${ref.name}`, text, ref.target) : escapeHtml(text);
    output += `<span data-literate-explore="${escapeHtml(ref.target)}">${link}</span>`;
    cursor = ref.end;
}
```

Exploration markers wrap only semantic uses. The client adds buttons; without
JavaScript there are no dead controls. Content-only templates include the same
inert payload and standalone initializer as the default template. Documents with
no usable reference uses emit neither, including declaration-only chapters and
annotation-only projects. Pending preview capabilities wait for the normal refresh.

```ts : <<screen exploration rendering>>=
export function renderScreenExploration(model: PublicationModel, path: string, format: ScreenFormat,
    destination?: PreviewDestination): string {
    const index = screenIndex(model);
    const targets = index.explorationTargets.get(path);
    if (!targets || !Array.from(targets).some(target => {
        const base = index.parts.get(index.families.get(target)?.contributions[0] || '');
        return base && screenHref(path, base.definition.path, target, format, destination) !== undefined;
    })) { return ''; }
    const payload = explorationPayload(model, path, format, destination);
    return `<script type="application/json" class="literate-exploration-data">${payload}</script>`
        + (format === 'html' ? `<script>${explorationBrowserScript()}</script>` : '');
}
```

Declaration labels link to their family's base. Every accepted contribution has
its own target; only the base carries the family target. Preview targets are
source-mapped `code-line` elements for local anchor/source synchronization.
Cross-file preview links instead use resolved capabilities; the handler calculates
current source positions when activated, rather than storing line numbers in URLs.
The host fence keeps its original token map and attributes as well.

```ts : <<screen contribution rendering>>=
export function renderScreenContribution(part: PublicationContribution, model: PublicationModel,
    format: ScreenFormat, label: string, code: string, destination?: PreviewDestination): string {
    const index = screenIndex(model);
    const family = index.families.get(part.fragmentTarget)!;
    const base = index.parts.get(family.contributions[0])!;
    const source = format === 'preview' ? ` class="code-line" data-line="${part.definition.line}"` : '';
    const familyAnchor = base.target === part.target ? `<span id="${family.target}"${source}></span>` : '';
    const heading = screenLink(screenHref(part.definition.path, base.definition.path, family.target, format, destination),
        `Definition of ${family.name}`, label, family.target);
    return `<div class="codefragment">\n<div class="fragmentname${format === 'preview' ? ' code-line' : ''}" id="${part.target}"${format === 'preview' ? ` data-line="${part.definition.line}"` : ''}>${familyAnchor}${heading}</div>\n`
        + `<div class="code">\n${code}\n</div>\n</div>`;
}
```

Native anchors are keyboard-focusable and carry descriptive accessible names.
Contribution anchors retain source mapping for the current preview. Fragment
references and declaration labels provide direct semantic navigation; contribution
blocks add no previous, base, or next chrome.

Immutable publication snapshots are indexed once and weakly cached, so references
and adapter lookups do not rescan the project. A fresh parse creates a fresh
snapshot; the weak cache does not retain closed projects.
Nested document maps avoid path/key delimiter collisions.

```ts : <<screen model index>>=
const screenIndexes = new WeakMap<PublicationModel, ReturnType<typeof buildScreenIndex>>();
const annotationIndexes = new WeakMap<PublicationAnnotations, WeakMap<PublicationModel, ReturnType<typeof buildScreenIndex>>>();
export function screenIndex(model: PublicationModel, annotations?: PublicationAnnotations) {
    let cache = screenIndexes;
    if (annotations) {
        if (!annotationIndexes.has(annotations)) { annotationIndexes.set(annotations, new WeakMap()); }
        cache = annotationIndexes.get(annotations)!;
    }
    let index = cache.get(model);
    if (!index) { index = buildScreenIndex(model, annotations); cache.set(model, index); }
    return index;
}
```

Build each lookup in one pass over families and contributions. Adapters share token
and line maps.

```ts : <<screen model index>>=+
function buildScreenIndex(model: PublicationModel, annotations?: PublicationAnnotations) {
    const families = new Map(model.fragments.map(family => [family.target, family]));
    const parts = new Map(model.contributions.map(part => [part.target, part]));
    const tokens = new Map<string, Map<number, PublicationContribution>>();
    const lines = new Map<string, Map<number, PublicationContribution>>();
    for (const part of model.contributions) {
        for (const [map, key] of [[tokens, part.tokenIndex], [lines, part.definition.line]] as const) {
            if (!map.has(part.definition.path)) { map.set(part.definition.path, new Map()); }
            map.get(part.definition.path)!.set(key, part);
        }
    }
    <<screen annotation index>>
    <<screen reverse references>>
    return { families, parts, tokens, lines, targets, annotationLines, annotationTargets,
        termReferences, fragmentReferences, explorationTargets };
}
```

Annotations extend the common destination map without changing publication parts.
Use locations remain explicit model data, including links inside annotation bodies.

```ts : <<screen annotation index>>=
    const targets = new Map<string, { definition: PublicationContribution['definition'] }>(parts);
    for (const family of model.fragments) {
        const base = parts.get(family.contributions[0]);
        if (base) { targets.set(family.target, base); }
    }
    const annotationLines = new Map<string, Map<number, PublicationTerm | PublicationNote>>();
    const annotationTargets = new Map<string, PublicationTerm | PublicationNote>();
    for (const annotation of [...annotations?.terms || [], ...annotations?.notes || []]) {
        targets.set(annotation.target, { definition: annotation.at });
        annotationTargets.set(annotation.target, annotation);
        if (!annotationLines.has(annotation.at.path)) { annotationLines.set(annotation.at.path, new Map()); }
        annotationLines.get(annotation.at.path)!.set(annotation.at.line, annotation);
    }
    for (const use of annotations?.uses || []) { targets.set(screenTermUseTarget(use), { definition: use.at }); }
```

Reverse references preserve encounter order and are built once per snapshot.
Repeated uses of one family in a contribution produce one index entry, without
rescanning every contribution for each family or every use for each term.

```ts : <<screen reverse references>>=
    const termReferences = new Map<string, PublicationTermUse[]>();
    for (const use of annotations?.uses || []) {
        if (!use.target) { continue; }
        if (!termReferences.has(use.target)) { termReferences.set(use.target, []); }
        termReferences.get(use.target)!.push(use);
    }
    const fragmentReferences = new Map<string, PublicationContribution[]>();
    const explorationTargets = new Map<string, Set<string>>();
    for (const part of model.contributions) {
        const seen = new Set<string>();
        for (const segment of part.segments) {
            if (segment.kind !== 'reference' || segment.resolution.kind !== 'resolved') { continue; }
            const target = segment.resolution.target;
            if (seen.has(target)) { continue; }
            seen.add(target);
            if (!fragmentReferences.has(target)) { fragmentReferences.set(target, []); }
            fragmentReferences.get(target)!.push(part);
            if (!parts.has(families.get(target)?.contributions[0] || '')) { continue; }
            const path = part.definition.path;
            if (!explorationTargets.has(path)) { explorationTargets.set(path, new Set()); }
            explorationTargets.get(path)!.add(target);
        }
    }
```

The preview host applies aliases before highlight.js. Our semantic overlay replaces
its highlighter, so apply the same compatibility mapping explicitly, without
changing the authored language or LaTeX policy. Unknown languages stay plain.

```ts : <<screen preview language aliases>>=
export function previewLanguage(language: string): string {
    switch (language.toLowerCase()) {
        case 'shell': return 'sh';
        case 'py3': return 'python';
        case 'tsx': case 'typescriptreact': return 'jsx';
        case 'json5': case 'jsonc': return 'json';
        case 'c#': case 'csharp': return 'cs';
        default: return language;
    }
}
```

Literal ranges and references arrive in source order. A monotonic syntax cursor
visits each syntax leaf once (plus split boundaries), rather than rescanning every
leaf per reference. A leaf straddling a reference remains available for its suffix.

```ts : <<screen syntax merge>>=
function screenSyntax(display: ReturnType<typeof highlightContribution>) {
    let index = 0;
    return (start: number, end: number): string => {
        const output: string[] = [];
        while (index < display.spans.length) {
            const span = display.spans[index];
            if (span.end <= start) { index++; continue; }
            if (span.start >= end) { break; }
            const left = Math.max(start, span.start), right = Math.min(end, span.end);
            const scope = span.scope.split('.')[0];
            if (left < right && scope !== 'comment') {
                const text = escapeHtml(display.text.slice(left, right));
                output.push(scope ? `<span class="hljs-${escapeHtml(scope)}">${text}</span>` : text);
            }
            if (span.end > end) { break; }
            index++;
        }
        return output.join('');
    };
}
```

## Annotation prose

Annotation bodies use the collector's parser, preserving reference aliases,
autolinks and occurrence offsets. A closed token vocabulary keeps raw HTML,
images, headings and code fences from introducing active elements or attributes.
The title is plain text; NOTE has its own numbered aside, never a term identity.

```ts : <<screen annotation rendering>>=
export interface ScreenAnnotationContext {
    model: PublicationModel; annotations: PublicationAnnotations; path: string;
    format: ScreenFormat; destination?: PreviewDestination;
}
const annotationMarkdown = new MarkdownIt({ html: false });
installScreenTermLinks(annotationMarkdown);
export function renderScreenAnnotation(annotation: PublicationTerm | PublicationNote,
    context: ScreenAnnotationContext): string {
    const term = 'title' in annotation;
    const tag = term ? 'section' : 'aside';
    const title = term ? annotation.title : `Note ${annotation.number}`;
    const source = context.format === 'preview' ? ` code-line" data-line="${annotation.at.line}` : '';
    const tokens = sanitizeAnnotationTokens(parseAnnotationBody(annotation.body));
    const literateUseTargets = screenTermUseAnchors(tokens, context.annotations, annotation.target);
    return `<${tag} class="literate-annotation literate-${term ? 'term' : 'note'}${source}" id="${annotation.target}">`
        + `<div class="literate-annotation-title">${escapeHtml(title)}</div>`
        + annotationMarkdown.renderer.render(tokens, annotationMarkdown.options,
                    { literateScreen: context, literateUseTargets }) + `</${tag}>\n`;
}
```

Sanitization preserves every token slot, so the collector's token and child
indexes still identify the rendered links. Rejected leaf content becomes escaped
text; rejected containers lose their tags, not their accepted inline children.
Only parser-owned link and ordered-list attributes survive.

```ts : <<screen annotation body sanitization>>=
const screenBodyTokens = new Set([
    'paragraph_open', 'paragraph_close', 'inline', 'text', 'softbreak', 'hardbreak',
    'bullet_list_open', 'bullet_list_close', 'ordered_list_open', 'ordered_list_close',
    'list_item_open', 'list_item_close', 'em_open', 'em_close', 'strong_open',
    'strong_close', 'code_inline', 'link_open', 'link_close'
]);
function sanitizeAnnotationTokens(tokens: Token[]): Token[] {
    for (const token of tokens) {
        if (!screenBodyTokens.has(token.type)) {
            token.type = 'text'; token.tag = ''; token.nesting = 0;
            token.children = null; token.hidden = false;
        } else if (token.children) { sanitizeAnnotationTokens(token.children); }
        token.attrs = token.attrs?.filter(([name]) =>
            (token.type === 'link_open' && (name === 'href' || name === 'title'))
            || (token.type === 'ordered_list_open' && name === 'start')) || null;
    }
    return tokens;
}
```

Term links share resolution in prose, headings and annotation bodies. A missing,
ambiguous or pending destination becomes a span, including its closing tag.
Semantic prose fragment tokens use the same family destinations as code references;
the `[rail]` suffix was consumed before rendering and does not affect screens.
Other links retain the host renderer's behavior and Markdown URL validation.

```ts : <<screen term links>>=
export function installScreenFragmentLinks(md: MarkdownIt): void {
    md.renderer.rules.literate_fragment_use = (tokens, index, _options, env) => {
        const token = tokens[index];
        const context = env?.literateScreen as ScreenAnnotationContext | undefined;
        const name = String(token.meta?.name || '');
        const family = context?.model.fragments.find(item => item.name === name);
        const lookup = context && screenIndex(context.model, context.annotations);
        const base = lookup?.parts.get(family?.contributions[0] || '');
        const href = context && family && base ? screenHref(context.path, base.definition.path,
            family.target, context.format, context.destination) : undefined;
        return family ? screenLink(href, `Definition of ${name}`, token.content, family.target) :
            escapeHtml(token.content);
    };
}
export function installScreenPublicationLinks(md: MarkdownIt): void {
    const open = md.renderer.rules.link_open, close = md.renderer.rules.link_close;
    md.renderer.rules.link_open = (tokens, index, options, env, renderer) => {
        const token = tokens[index], href = token.attrGet('href') || '';
        const hash = href.indexOf('#');
        const reference = hash >= 0 ? parsePublicationReference(href.slice(hash + 1)) : undefined;
        if (!reference) {
            const context = env?.literateScreen as ScreenAnnotationContext | undefined;
            if (!/^(?:[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:|\/\/|\/)/i.test(href)) {
                if (context?.format === 'html') {
                    token.attrSet('href', href.replace(/\.literate(?=[?#]|$)/, '.html'));
                } else if (context?.format === 'preview') {
                    const source = href.split(/[?#]/)[0].replace(/\.html$/, '.literate');
                    let path: string;
                    try { path = posix.normalize(posix.join(posix.dirname(context.path), decodeURIComponent(source))); }
                    catch { path = ''; }
                    if (context.model.documents.some(document => document.path === path)) {
                        token.attrSet('href', href.replace(/\.html(?=[?#]|$)/, '.literate'));
                    }
                }
            }
            try { return open ? open(tokens, index, options, env, renderer) : renderer.renderToken(tokens, index, options); }
            finally { token.attrSet('href', href); }
        }
        const context = env?.literateScreen as ScreenAnnotationContext | undefined;
        const target = publicationReferenceTarget(reference);
        const document = href.slice(0, hash);
        let resolved: string | undefined;
        if (context && (!document || posix.normalize(posix.join(posix.dirname(context.path), document)) === context.path)) {
            resolved = context.format === 'preview' ? context.destination?.(target) : `#${target}`;
        } else if (context?.format === 'html' && document.endsWith('.literate')) {
            resolved = document.slice(0, -'.literate'.length) + `.html#${target}`;
        }
        token.meta = { ...(token.meta || {}), literatePublicationLink: !!resolved };
        return resolved ? `<a href="${escapeHtml(resolved)}" data-literate-target="${target}">`
            : '<span class="literate-publication-unresolved">';
    };
    md.renderer.rules.link_close = (tokens, index, options, env, renderer) => {
        let start = index - 1;
        while (start >= 0 && tokens[start].type !== 'link_open') { start--; }
        if (start >= 0) {
            const href = tokens[start].attrGet('href') || '', hash = href.indexOf('#');
            if (hash >= 0 && parsePublicationReference(href.slice(hash + 1))) {
                return tokens[start].meta?.literatePublicationLink ? '</a>' : '</span>';
            }
        }
        return close ? close(tokens, index, options, env, renderer) : renderer.renderToken(tokens, index, options);
    };
}
export function installScreenTermLinks(md: MarkdownIt): void {
    const open = md.renderer.rules.link_open, close = md.renderer.rules.link_close;
    const destination = (href: string, context?: ScreenAnnotationContext) => {
        if (!context) { return undefined; }
        const target = resolveTermHref(context.annotations, href);
        const at = target && screenIndex(context.model, context.annotations).targets.get(target)?.definition;
        return target && at ? { target, href: screenHref(context.path, at.path, target,
            context.format, context.destination) } : undefined;
    };
    md.renderer.rules.link_open = (tokens, index, options, env, renderer) => {
        const href = tokens[index].attrGet('href') || '';
        if (!href.startsWith('term:')) { return open ? open(tokens, index, options, env, renderer) : renderer.renderToken(tokens, index, options); }
        const link = destination(href, env?.literateScreen);
        const use = env?.literateUseTargets?.get(tokens[index]) as PublicationTermUse | undefined;
        const anchor = use ? `<span id="${screenTermUseTarget(use)}"${env.literateScreen?.format === 'preview' ? ` class="code-line" data-line="${use.at.line}"` : ''}></span>` : '';
        return anchor + (link?.href ? `<a href="${escapeHtml(link.href)}" data-literate-target="${link.target}">` : '<span class="literate-term-unresolved">');
    };
    md.renderer.rules.link_close = (tokens, index, options, env, renderer) => {
        let start = index - 1;
        while (start >= 0 && tokens[start].type !== 'link_open') { start--; }
        const href = start >= 0 ? tokens[start].attrGet('href') || '' : '';
        if (href.startsWith('term:')) { return destination(href, env?.literateScreen)?.href ? '</a>' : '</span>'; }
        return close ? close(tokens, index, options, env, renderer) : renderer.renderToken(tokens, index, options);
    };
}
```

Use anchors address model occurrences rather than matching label text. They are
separate from definition identities and share the capability location map.

```ts : <<screen term use anchors>>=
function screenTermUseTarget(use: PublicationTermUse): string {
    const owner = use.owner.split('').map(char => char.charCodeAt(0).toString(16).padStart(4, '0')).join('');
    return `annotation-use-${owner}-${use.tokenIndex}-${use.childIndex}`;
}
export function screenTermUseAnchors(tokens: Token[], annotations: PublicationAnnotations, owner: string) {
    const anchors = new Map<Token, PublicationTermUse>();
    for (const use of annotations.uses) {
        if (use.owner !== owner) { continue; }
        const token = tokens[use.tokenIndex]?.children?.[use.childIndex];
        if (token?.type === 'link_open' && token.attrGet('href')?.startsWith('term:')) { anchors.set(token, use); }
    }
    return anchors;
}
```

## Screen indexes

Only annotated books gain indexes. One root page owns both lists, with global
navigation to definitions and accepted fragment contributions. Entries use titles
and explicit semantic identities, not guessed identifiers or print page numbers.

```ts : <<screen book indexes>>=
export function renderScreenIndexes(context: ScreenAnnotationContext, paths: readonly string[]): string {
    const { model, annotations, path, format, destination } = context;
    if (!annotations.terms.length && !annotations.notes.length) { return ''; }
    if (path !== (paths.includes('index.literate') ? 'index.literate' : paths[0])) { return ''; }
    const lookup = screenIndex(model, annotations);
    const link = (target: string, title: string) => {
        const at = lookup.targets.get(target)?.definition;
        return screenLink(at && screenHref(path, at.path, target, format, destination), title, title, target);
    };
    const terms = annotations.terms.map(term => `<li>${link(term.target, term.title)} — Definition`
        + (lookup.termReferences.get(term.target) || []).map((use, index) =>
            ` · ${link(screenTermUseTarget(use), `Reference ${index + 1}`)}`).join('') + '</li>').join('');
    const fragments = model.fragments.map(family => `<li>${link(family.target, family.name)}: `
        + family.contributions.map((target, index) => link(target, index ? `Addition ${index}` : 'Definition')).join(' · ')
        + (lookup.fragmentReferences.get(family.target) || [])
            .map((part, index) => ` · ${link(part.target, `Reference ${index + 1}`)}`).join('') + '</li>').join('');
    return `<nav class="literate-screen-index" aria-label="Term index"><h2>Term index</h2><ul>${terms}</ul></nav>`
        + `<nav class="literate-screen-index" aria-label="Fragment index"><h2>Fragment index</h2><ul>${fragments}</ul></nav>`;
}
```

Annotation presentation is scoped to document wrappers and inherits the active
palette. It does not add generated theme data or change legacy book styling.

```ts : <<screen annotation styles>>=
export const screenStyle = `.literate-document .literate-annotation { margin-block: 1em; padding: .6em 1em; border-inline-start: .2em solid currentColor; }
.literate-document .literate-annotation-title { font-weight: bold; }
.literate-document .literate-note { border-inline-start-style: dotted; }
.literate-document .literate-term-unresolved { text-decoration: underline dotted; }
.literate-document .literate-screen-index { margin-block-start: 2em; }`;
```

The generated screen-publication and installed-host regression implementations are
collected in
[Appendix H — Testing and verification reference](appendix_testing_verification.html).
